 for detecting doping agents in hair</article-title><source>Forensic Science International</source><volume>107</volume><fpage>335</fpage><lpage>45</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10689585</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="b28-bmi-2009-001"><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Weger</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Schlake</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name></person-group><year>2005</year><article-title>IGF-1 signalling controls the hair growth cycle in the differentiation of hair shafts</article-title><source>J. Invest. Dermatol</source><volume>125</volume><fpage>873</fpage><lpage>82</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16297183</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-group><fig id="f1-bmi-2009-001" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Significant gene expression regulations in cell culture 1. The ratio was calculated by 2<sup>&#x00394;&#x00394;Ct</sup> whereby the control was set 1 (upper control line). Data are depicted in bars + standard deviations. Red crosses (*) mark the significantly regulated target genes.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="bmi-2009-001f1"/></fig><fig id="f2-bmi-2009-001" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Significant gene expression regulations in cell culture 2. The ratio was calculated by 2<sup>&#x00394;&#x00394;Ct</sup> whereby the control was set 1 (upper control line). Data are depicted in bars + standard deviation. Red crosses (*) mark the significantly regulated target genes.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="bmi-2009-001f2"/></fig><table-wrap id="t1-bmi-2009-001" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>List of used primer pairs, showing the length of the amplicon (nt), the melt temperature (Tm in &#x000b0;C) and the accession number of the sequence.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Group</th><th align="left